@*
    Routing lives in Shared, not in a head, so both heads route the same screens.
    AppAssembly is this library's — a head adding its own routable page would pass
    it through AdditionalAssemblies rather than moving this file.
*@
@*
    The ErrorBoundary is here rather than inside the layout so it catches a failure in the
    layout itself, which is where the shell loads the session and the app bar — and that is
    exactly where an unhandled exception shows up as a bare "Something went wrong" bar with
    the detail nowhere.

    On a device that detail went nowhere at all: Blazor Hybrid does not route it through
    ILogger the way Blazor Server does, so a file logger caught nothing. Rendering it is the
    only place it reliably appears.

    DEBUG only. A release build shows the plain apology — an exception message on a front
    desk screen tells a receptionist nothing and may name internals.
*@
<ErrorBoundary @ref="_boundary">
    <ChildContent>
        <Router AppAssembly="typeof(Routes).Assembly" NotFoundPage="typeof(Shared.Layout.NotFound)">
            <Found Context="routeData">
                <RouteView RouteData="routeData" DefaultLayout="typeof(Shared.Layout.MainLayout)" />
                <FocusOnNavigate RouteData="routeData" Selector="h2" />
            </Found>
        </Router>
    </ChildContent>
    <ErrorContent Context="failure">
        <div class="m-6 border-2 border-accent bg-accent-100 p-4 text-sm text-accent-800"
             role="alert">
            <strong>Something went wrong.</strong>

            @*
                A C# flag, not #if. A preprocessor directive in Razor MARKUP is not a
                directive at all — it is literal text, and the page would render the words
                "#if DEBUG" to the user. Directives only work inside @code.
            *@
            @if (ShowDetail)
            {
                <pre class="mt-3 overflow-auto text-[11px] whitespace-pre-wrap">@Describe(failure)</pre>
            }

            <button class="btn btn-primary mt-3" @onclick="Recover">Try again</button>
        </div>
    </ErrorContent>
</ErrorBoundary>

@code {
    private ErrorBoundary? _boundary;

    /// <summary>Whether to show the exception rather than only the apology.</summary>
#if DEBUG
    private const bool ShowDetail = true;
#else
    private const bool ShowDetail = false;
#endif

    /// <summary>Clears the error so the next render is attempted rather than the apology.</summary>
    private void Recover() => _boundary?.Recover();

    /// <summary>
    /// The whole chain, innermost last.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Every inner exception, because the useful one is almost never the outer: "failed to
    /// activate X" says nothing, and the inner "unable to resolve service for type Y" names
    /// the thing actually missing.
    /// </remarks>
    private static string Describe(Exception failure)
    {
        var text = new System.Text.StringBuilder();

        for (var inner = failure; inner is not null; inner = inner.InnerException)
        {
            text.AppendLine($"{inner.GetType().FullName}: {inner.Message}");

            if (inner.StackTrace is { Length: > 0 } stack) text.AppendLine(stack);

            text.AppendLine();
        }

        return text.ToString();
    }
}
